WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.090
So, we lost one of the most
important people in our family,

2
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.580
who's made a huge, huge,
huge impact on a lot of us.

3
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:23.550
So Moshe and I were very close
for many, many, many years.

4
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:30.650
So, in the army. This is back in the late

5
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:37.550
'60s, early '70s. And this
is around the time when

6
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:43.850
I would be in Jerusalem, going
and helping Moshe and the

7
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:49.060
Rondo. This is like '72,
to get this place started.

8
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:57.530
This is the Café Rondo.
And we just had many,

9
00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:01.460
many nights that we spent together.

10
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.880
When we go to Israel, needless
to say, it was the Rondo,

11
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.660
because we didn't know how to go to
Israel without going to the Rondo.

12
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.070
Saying jet lag was never a problem,
because we would be partying till five

13
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.580
o'clock in the morning in the Rondo.

14
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:23.020
The Rondo was Moshe. That
was his home away from home.

15
00:01:23.368 --> 00:01:30.138
After I proposed to
Dikla, he made a point of

16
00:01:30.188 --> 00:01:35.768
flying to St. Louis to meet my
family before she gave me an answer.

17
00:01:36.208 --> 00:01:39.878
He wanted to check out
the family here in St.

18
00:01:39.928 --> 00:01:46.818
Louis. I wasn't just a bum
somewhere, you know, that Siona

19
00:01:46.868 --> 00:01:53.078
and Ben Zion would regret
that their daughter made

20
00:01:53.128 --> 00:01:56.498
a decision like that. So he came to St.

21
00:01:56.548 --> 00:01:59.428
Louis and stayed with
my mother and father.

22
00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:06.118
They had a wonderful time
together, and I assume he reported

23
00:02:06.168 --> 00:02:07.648
everything was good.

24
00:02:08.017 --> 00:02:12.597
And I met this man who
was larger than life.

25
00:02:13.417 --> 00:02:19.067
I mean, he was the most
extraordinary guy I had ever met,

26
00:02:19.117 --> 00:02:25.227
because I didn't know people that
could drink and smoke and laugh

27
00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:30.997
and party like Moshe. I had
never met anybody like that.

28
00:02:31.677 --> 00:02:37.897
He was just in a class by himself.

29
00:02:38.897 --> 00:02:43.557
Never apologetic, but
always there for you.

30
00:02:43.802 --> 00:02:47.702
Only maybe net new is, I feel like
he was kind of frozen in time.

31
00:02:47.702 --> 00:02:51.102
Like that picture Mom shared from the '80s,
from the early '80s.

32
00:02:51.102 --> 00:02:56.022
That's my image of him from the '90s,
from the 2000s and the 2010s.

33
00:02:56.022 --> 00:03:00.942
I don't know if the Black Label just cured him
and he was just preserved.

34
00:03:00.942 --> 00:03:05.982
He was pickled for the last 30, 40 years.

35
00:03:06.472 --> 00:03:10.462
And I feel like I have this vivid
recollection that he told me he doesn't

36
00:03:10.512 --> 00:03:16.102
eat dinner. He stops eating at like
four o'clock, or like a late lunch,

37
00:03:16.152 --> 00:03:19.392
and that was the last meal he
has for the rest of the day.

38
00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:23.312
And it was such a weird
memory, but it stuck with me.

39
00:03:23.432 --> 00:03:27.272
You know, that was probably over 20
years ago he told me that story.

40
00:03:27.592 --> 00:03:29.712
And I just remember, I'm like, oh
my God, someone doesn't eat dinner.

41
00:03:29.852 --> 00:03:32.922
I think that was just so
wild for me to process.

42
00:03:32.972 --> 00:03:34.502
Food is so amazing. He has a restaurant.

43
00:03:34.552 --> 00:03:38.002
He doesn't eat dinner.
And so I remember that.

44
00:03:38.432 --> 00:03:40.482
We were in Florida for Passover one year.

45
00:03:40.532 --> 00:03:44.322
I guess this was probably every year
growing up, but we were at Shlomi and

46
00:03:44.372 --> 00:03:49.222
Debbie's house, and I just remember
his laughter roaring through the entire

47
00:03:49.272 --> 00:03:51.682
house, almost to the point
where I was a little concerned,

48
00:03:51.732 --> 00:03:57.902
like, okay, like, loud. And
that specific memory of him

49
00:03:57.952 --> 00:04:02.622
has been very on par with what everyone
has said, and I think it's pretty

50
00:04:02.672 --> 00:04:05.712
amazing that that's the
legacy that is left behind.

51
00:04:05.813 --> 00:04:08.053
Michael: If you give me the
line, I can do the voice.

52
00:04:09.273 --> 00:04:13.353
Jonathan: The world's most interesting
man has been going on for a while.

53
00:04:14.833 --> 00:04:17.903
And he doesn't always drink
beer, but when he drinks beer,

54
00:04:17.953 --> 00:04:26.353
it's Dos Equis. And so I couldn't help
myself by saying I don't drink whiskey,

55
00:04:28.073 --> 00:04:34.393
Michael: I don't drink whiskey,
but when I do, it's Johnnie Walker.

56
00:04:34.773 --> 00:04:37.223
Ruthie: Oh, that's good, Mikey.

57
00:04:37.493 --> 00:04:39.983
Michael: Yes. I've been training
for this moment all of my life.

58
00:04:40.119 --> 00:04:43.379
I'm in Jerusalem, there
for my bar mitzvah.

59
00:04:43.519 --> 00:04:45.899
Moshe is hosting at the Rondo.

60
00:04:46.899 --> 00:04:53.209
We go for like a pre-party
visit to see what we're

61
00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:54.719
going to do, the food, the drinks.

62
00:04:54.939 --> 00:04:59.909
He lines me up with a Coca-Cola,
some chips, some hummus,

63
00:04:59.959 --> 00:05:05.239
and I'm just like, yes, this
is all a kid needs, I'm set.

64
00:05:05.829 --> 00:05:13.329
And being at the Rondo, of course, he gave me
my first job at like 10 years old.

65
00:05:13.329 --> 00:05:14.969
And it really wasn't much of a job.

66
00:05:14.969 --> 00:05:19.269
All I had to do was open a bottle of Coca-Cola,
classic glass bottle,

67
00:05:19.269 --> 00:05:21.609
pop the top, and he gave me 20 shekels.

68
00:05:21.609 --> 00:05:25.029
And I will never forget that,
because that was like, that was...

69
00:05:25.029 --> 00:05:27.409
I thought I had a million dollars
in my pocket.

70
00:05:27.607 --> 00:05:31.357
He had this saying, which I've never
heard anyone else in the world ever

71
00:05:31.407 --> 00:05:35.307
say. He would say, what a beautiful.

72
00:05:36.487 --> 00:05:43.337
What a beautiful. So it's literally
translating yofi, but in his

73
00:05:43.387 --> 00:05:47.227
own world. What a beautiful thing.

74
00:05:48.467 --> 00:05:53.377
And that was him. He just
was a joy to be around.

75
00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:59.740
I want to take that one step further
and encourage you, each one of you,

76
00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.880
including myself and our growing children:

77
00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:08.140
any memory that we have of him,
let us try to honor and be like him.

78
00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:14.020
Let us try to be good and always open,
whether it's our homes, whether it's our arms.

79
00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:17.180
And obviously many of us, all of us,
are already doing this.

80
00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.500
My point is, look for opportunities in life

81
00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.240
to honor them in memory
and to honor them in action.

82
00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.310
And I think it's such a
neat representation of:

83
00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:32.430
he was driven. He had that drive in
life, and he knew where we're going.

84
00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.730
We're going to get there. He's
going to show us the best places.

85
00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:37.920
And this is Hadera. So I
wanted to share that with you.

86
00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.360
That's Moshe's arm.

87
00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:46.130
Debbie: You know, it's interesting,
when I see that picture,

88
00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:51.250
I look at the way the light is reflecting,
and that's how I think of Moshe.

89
00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:57.730
He reflected light. He brought
light, his personality, and

90
00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:02.360
just his persona. I mean, that's
what I see in that picture.

91
00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:03.790
The light, it just comes.

92
00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:10.330
Tali: Moshe, I love you. May your life
and legacy and energy continue to live on.

93
00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:16.230
And I'm always thinking of you
in my heart. Love you.

94
00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:21.230
Shlomi: Moshe, I miss you. I know you're
looking at all of us from over there.

95
00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:23.330
Rest in peace.

96
00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:25.530
Galia: Love you, Dod Moshe.

97
00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:30.930
Keep doing where you are, what you have meant,
what you managed to do here on earth.

98
00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:39.730
Ruthie (in Hebrew): My dear uncle,
I will never forget you.

99
00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:48.930
Thank you for what you were in my life.
And may you rest in peace forever and ever.

100
00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:54.910
And here's to Uncle Moshe, who would
always call me every year on my birthday,

101
00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.160
or actually any time, and sing to me:

102
00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:06.490
I love you so much, Daniel. And
I hope you all get to feel that

103
00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:10.610
love, because to each and every
one of you, I love you so much.
